<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen W. Kearny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen W. Kearny was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army officer who commanded the Army of the West in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>. In August 1846 he occupied Santa Fe without a battle and proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> American territory before marching on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, where he fought at San Pasqual and served briefly as military governor. He died in 1848 after further service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-w-kearny.jpg" alt="Stephen W. Kearny" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Engraved by T. B. Welch, expressly for Graham's Magazine, July 1849. Peter A. Juley &amp; Son, photographers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stephen_W._Kearny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephen Watts Kearny was born on 30 August 1794 in Newark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, the fifteenth and youngest child of Philip III and Susanna Watts Kearny; his father, of Irish ancestry, was a wine merchant and landowner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth-amboy">Perth Amboy</a>. He studied for two years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> militia as an ensign in 1812. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> he was commissioned a first lieutenant in the 13th Infantry. At the Battle of Queenston Heights on 13 October 1812 he and Lieutenant Colonel Winfield Scott led a charge that took the British position, but the militia failed to reinforce them. Wounded and captured, Kearny spent months in captivity, which hardened his prejudice against militias.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the war he served on the western frontier under General Henry Atkinson, on the Yellowstone Expedition of 1819 and the 1825 expedition that reached the mouth of the Yellowstone River. In 1826 he became the first commander of Jefferson Barracks at St. Louis, and in the late 1820s he married Mary Radford, stepdaughter of William Clark; they had eleven children, six of whom died in childhood. In 1833 he became second in command of the new 1st Dragoon Regiment, from which the U.S. Cavalry later grew, and its colonel in 1836. In 1845 he led the dragoons on a reconnaissance of over 2,000 miles to South Pass and back in 99 days.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, after San Pasqual, a force of about 600 men including his dragoons retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> on 10 January 1847. Kearny then quarrelled with Commodore Robert F. Stockton over command, since the War Department had set no rule for it, and Stockton made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-c-fremont">John C. Frémont</a> military governor. Once reinforcements arrived, Kearny took command and had Frémont court-martialed for insubordination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kearny was later governor of Veracruz and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and in September 1848 he received a brevet promotion to major general over the opposition of Senator Thomas Hart Benton, Frémont's father-in-law. He caught yellow fever in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and died in St. Louis on 31 October 1848, aged 54. The historian Allan Nevins, writing of the quarrel with Frémont, called him &quot;a stern-tempered soldier who made few friends and many enemies&quot;. Kearny, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, Kearney, Nebraska, Fort Kearny and Kearny Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephen W. Kearny” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>American troops under <b>Stephen Kearny</b> occupied Santa Fe in 1846, and the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo ceded it to the United States in 1848.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Newark, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in St. Louis, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.74,-74.17&amp;year=1829" aria-label="Newark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2732 709 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stephen W. Kearny"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3182 979h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3182 979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.74,-74.17&amp;year=1829">What was here in 1829? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
